EFS (Elastic File System)
managed NFS/file system that can be mounted on many EC2 instances at the same time
works across multiple AZ/Availability Zones
uses NFSv4.1
mainly used with Linux workloads
POSIX-compliant file system
automatically scales storage as files are added/removed
uses security groups for network access
encryption at rest using KMS + encryption in transit using TLS
shared file storage for web serving, content management, data sharing, containers, etc.

Performance modes:

General Purpose → default, low latency
Max I/O → high aggregate throughput, higher latency

Throughput modes:

Bursting → throughput based on amount of data stored, can burst
Provisioned → specify the required throughput
Elastic → automatically scales throughput based on workload

Storage classes:

Standard → frequently accessed data
EFS-IA → infrequently accessed data, lower storage cost + retrieval cost
Archive → rarely accessed data, lowest storage cost + retrieval cost

Lifecycle policies:
automatically move files between Standard → IA → Archive based on access patterns
